 ("F9", "Comparative analysis", "What a program may fetch and with what identity is a question the chapter touches only through bandwidth, through the advice to prefer an API and through the secrecy of the key: the Robots Exclusion Protocol of RFC 9309 states that its rules are not a form of access authorization, so a robots.txt is a request that a polite crawler honours and the standard library can read with urllib.robotparser (which exposed Crawl-delay and Request-rate, and a crawler that read them waited between its pages); an API key identifies an account, the fake key of the chapter's example was answered by OpenWeather with status 401 and a JSON message on 2026-10-12, the free plan allows 60 calls a minute, and the weather service api.weather.gov, which the chapter names, answered with forecast periods and no key but documents that a User-Agent identifying the application is required.", ["P28", "P05", "P01", "P35", "P36", "P37"]),
